"""Original capstone policy briefing and reform exercise."""


def build(c, m):
    return c(77, 'Policy Briefing and Reform Practicum', 'Policy Briefing\nand Reform\nPracticum',
        'Produce a concise fictional policy brief and staged reform proposal with traceable claims, feasible delivery and a reasoned evaluation plan.', [
        m('Frame a decision and build a claim record', 'Separate a policy decision request from its facts, assumptions and evidence limits.', [
            'A policy brief helps a decision owner understand a bounded problem and a reasoned choice. It should identify what decision is requested, why the issue matters now and what information supports the request. Do not begin with a preferred slogan and search only for confirming sources. In the fictional practicum, use the supplied records to distinguish a policy promise from a delivery observation and a proposed explanation. A global social-protection synthesis can inform questions about coverage and adequacy, but it is not a local entitlement decision or a current estimate for the invented population. [S51]',
            'Create an original claim record with a short statement, its source, relevant date, study or record type, and the limit on interpretation. Publication date may differ from the period observed, and a newer paper can analyse older records. The US cash-transfer trial addresses a particular population, payment comparison and set of outcomes; it does not guarantee every family result or direct transfer to a local service. Describe the outcome needed for the brief rather than citing a journal rank as if it validated the proposed recommendation. [R61]',
            'Contextual sources can help develop questions when their limits are explicit. The Nigerian digital-budget case uses abductive reasoning and proposes mechanisms; its accessible indexed sections do not establish a national causal effect. A brief may therefore use it to ask whether information connects to response capacity, while keeping any effectiveness claim unproven. If a full source cannot be accessed, state what was actually read and avoid pretending to have appraised its complete methods. A bibliography should allow the reader to locate the source, not create an appearance of certainty beyond the inspected material. [R64]',
            'Arrange the brief around the decision, not around every fact collected. Include the problem, a small set of relevant options, the recommended next step and the main uncertainty that could change the choice. Identify which institution or role must verify authority before action. Participation guidance reinforces the importance of explaining what decisions are open and how contributions will be used. Keep the classroom exercise fictional and non-personal; do not collect confidential records, promise public expenditure or present the brief as an approved government instruction. [S53]'],
            'A fictional access programme has an announced entitlement, incomplete receipt figures and an unstaffed response inbox. Its draft brief cites a 2026 publication as if its data were collected that year and demands immediate national adoption.',
            'Write a bounded decision request and three claim-record entries. Separate observed facts, contextual propositions and assumptions; identify one date or access limit.',
            'A strong brief asks for a reviewable step supported by the available record. A publication year does not establish a data year, and a contextual proposition is not a measured national effect.', ['S51', 'R61', 'R64', 'S53'], [
                ('A brief should identify a bounded decision.', 'Which opening best fits the fictional practicum?', 'Request a defined review of the access and response gap by a responsible role.', ['Demand national adoption without checking authority.', 'Begin with an unrelated list of every source.', 'Promise all households a new entitlement.'], 'A bounded request connects the problem, evidence and decision owner.'),
                ('Claim records distinguish source dates and observation periods.', 'A paper published in 2026 analyses older records. What should the brief show?', 'Both publication date and the relevant data period.', ['That all data were necessarily collected in 2026.', 'Only the latest date to appear current.', 'No dates because they complicate the story.'], 'Publication timing and the period studied answer different questions.'),
                ('Evidence access limits should be disclosed.', 'Only indexed sections of a source were inspected. What is accurate?', 'State the inspected sections and avoid a whole-methods appraisal claim.', ['Claim the entire paper was reviewed.', 'Invent missing methods.', 'Treat a browser restriction as confirmation of validity.'], 'A traceable brief reports the extent of its actual source appraisal.'),
                ('A journal rank cannot replace outcome appraisal.', 'What should accompany the US trial citation?', 'Its relevant comparison, outcome and transfer limitations.', ['Only a Q1 label.', 'A guarantee of identical local effects.', 'A claim that every family measure improved.'], 'Article-specific evidence matters more than a ranking shorthand.'),
                ('Factual observations and hypotheses should be separate.', 'An invented inbox is unstaffed. Which claim stays within evidence?', 'Response capacity is a plausible issue requiring a defined check.', ['Every delay is intentional concealment.', 'A new portal will certainly end corruption.', 'All national services share the same defect.'], 'A local observation can motivate a bounded hypothesis without proving universal motives or effects.')]),
        m('Compare options and stage a feasible reform', 'Connect an option to authority, resources, consultation and a controlled learning step.', [
            'An options section should compare a small number of plausible responses against the same purpose and constraints. For the fictional inbox problem, one option could clarify responsibility and processing capacity, while another could redesign an access channel. Neither is complete if it ignores the other part of delivery. State the mechanism, resource need, authority dependency and uncertainty for each. The Nigerian digital-budget case is a contextual prompt to examine the connection between accessible information and institutional response, not evidence that technology alone guarantees public value or eliminates every barrier. [R64]',
            'A staged proposal begins with a verification step before an implementation commitment. Confirm the responsible institution, the decision still open, the relevant non-personal record and the resources required. Then propose a limited learning activity with an explicit review point. A pilot is not a way to bypass authority or an automatic promise of later expansion. Institutional accountability depends on mandates and follow-up, so the proposal must show which role can approve a change and which role will explain what happened after approval. Learners can recommend this sequence without exercising those powers themselves. [S54]',
            'Consultation belongs where input can still influence the design. Explain the choice, identify missing perspectives, use appropriate accessible methods and promise only feedback the team can provide. OECD guidance helps frame these questions without requiring a copied source checklist. A small fictional exercise might compare opening-hour alternatives or wording of an explanation, while leaving statutory entitlement outside scope. Describe how contributions will be summarised and how the decision owner will explain accepted, declined or unresolved suggestions; do not claim that one meeting represents every intended user. [S53]',
            'Record a limited implementation sequence with dependencies and a review observation. For example, verify the mandate, assign an authorised response owner, test an accessible explanation using invented enquiries and review whether a reasoned response is documented. This is an educational proposal, not permission to contact real recipients or collect confidential complaints. State the condition for pausing or modifying the proposal if capacity is absent. A feasible reform note makes both the next action and the evidence needed for a further decision visible, instead of presenting a large uncosted promise as completed progress. [S54]'],
            'A fictional reform team wants to buy a new portal before deciding who answers questions. Its proposed consultation begins after procurement is final and promises that every suggestion will be implemented.',
            'Compare two options using common headings. Propose four stages, including authority verification, meaningful input, a limited learning step and review.',
            'Clarify response responsibility before treating technology as the solution. Consultation needs an honest open choice, and expansion should depend on verified authority, capacity and evidence.', ['R64', 'S54', 'S53'], [
                ('Options should address the identified mechanism.', 'What is missing from buying the fictional portal before assigning responses?', 'A functioning institutional responsibility and capacity to act.', ['A guarantee that a purchase resolves all enquiries.', 'Proof that no consultation is useful.', 'A reason to remove every alternative.'], 'A channel cannot substitute for the organisation needed to use it.'),
                ('Staging begins with authority and feasibility.', 'What should precede the fictional implementation commitment?', 'Verification of the responsible mandate and required resources.', ['A publicity claim of completed success.', 'Unapproved collection of private records.', 'A learner signature authorising public expenditure.'], 'A proposal needs an authorised operating basis before action.'),
                ('Consultation must concern an honest open choice.', 'Procurement is final but the fictional invitation promises co-design of that purchase. What correction is needed?', 'Explain what remains open and seek input only on meaningful choices.', ['Pretend the closed choice is still undecided.', 'Promise every suggestion will be implemented.', 'Hide the decision owner.'], 'The invitation must match the real scope for influence.'),
                ('A limited pilot needs a review condition.', 'Which statement strengthens the fictional pilot proposal?', 'Specify an observation and conditions for continuation, modification or pause.', ['Expansion is guaranteed regardless of results.', 'No record will be kept.', 'A pilot bypasses every operating rule.'], 'A learning step should inform a further authorised decision.'),
                ('Common headings make trade-offs visible.', 'Which options table is useful for the fictional decision?', 'Mechanism, resources, authority, access and uncertainty for each option.', ['Costs for one option and only slogans for the other.', 'An invented effect estimate where none is known.', 'A rank based solely on the newest citation.'], 'Consistent criteria expose differences without concealing evidence gaps.')]),
        m('Write an accountable recommendation and review plan', 'Communicate progress honestly and specify evidence that could change a recommendation.', [
            'The final recommendation should tell a decision owner what to do next, why that step fits the evidence and what still needs verification. Keep implementation status precise. A proposed action, an accepted recommendation, a completed change and a demonstrated outcome are different statements. The OECD public-audit summary distinguishes institutional outputs and outcomes, and control from influence. Use those distinctions to avoid claiming that the publication of a brief or acceptance of its recommendation has itself improved services. An honest recommendation can be useful while its final effects remain uncertain. [S55]',
            'Specify a review question with a measure, denominator, period and source. An invented response process might track reasoned dispositions out of all comparable enquiries, while a later access question examines whether intended users can obtain the service. Explain missing data and alternative influences. The review should not quietly replace the original target with an easier count after seeing the results. Keep a dated version history for any justified adaptation, including the reason and approving role, so the record supports fair comparison rather than a retrospective success narrative. [S55]',
            'A research-informed conclusion must preserve the limits of its external evidence. The US transfer trial does not show broad improvement on every self-reported family measure, and its setting differs from the fictional service. The Nigerian digital-budget inquiry develops propositions rather than a measured corruption-reduction guarantee. State how those sources informed the question and where local evidence is still needed. Do not borrow a confident headline, a journal quartile or a broad normative recommendation to fill a gap that the actual study design cannot resolve. [R61] [R64]',
            'Close with an explanation plan rather than a ceremonial declaration of success. Name who should communicate the decision, how relevant contributions and unresolved concerns will be represented, and what observation would prompt reconsideration. Participation guidance highlights feedback and evaluation as parts of a meaningful process. The practicum submission should therefore include a concise brief, a claim record, an options comparison and a staged review plan using original fictional material. Completion demonstrates learning; it does not authorise public policy, replace a professional mandate or unlock school certification without the separate required assessment and signature gates. [S53]'],
            'A fictional brief is accepted but no change has occurred. Its author reports a completed reform, deletes the original access target and substitutes the number of downloads as proof of impact.',
            'Write a 150-word recommendation and a four-entry review plan. Correct status and attribution, preserve the original target and name evidence that would alter the recommendation.',
            'Acceptance is not implementation. Downloads are a dissemination count; retain the access question and versioned changes, and communicate what is verified and what remains uncertain.', ['S55', 'R61', 'R64', 'S53'], [
                ('Acceptance and completed reform are different states.', 'A fictional brief is accepted with no implemented change. What status is accurate?', 'Recommendation accepted; implementation not yet evidenced.', ['Reform completed with proven impact.', 'Universal service access achieved.', 'All review requirements satisfied.'], 'An accepted proposal is not a documented operational change.'),
                ('Dissemination measures do not prove final impact.', 'What do fictional download counts directly establish?', 'Recorded downloads within the stated measurement limits.', ['A proven causal improvement in access.', 'Universal reading and agreement.', 'Completed public accountability in every institution.'], 'A dissemination count does not demonstrate behaviour or service outcomes.'),
                ('Changing targets requires visible history.', 'An invented team changes its access target after seeing results. What must be retained?', 'The original target, reason, date and authorised revision.', ['Only the easier replacement measure.', 'A claim that both targets were always identical.', 'No record of the change.'], 'A version history prevents misleading retrospective comparison.'),
                ('A recommendation should identify disconfirming evidence.', 'What makes a fictional recommendation open to review?', 'State an observation that would justify modification or reconsideration.', ['Declare it correct regardless of every result.', 'Hide evidence gaps.', 'Replace the review with a publication celebration.'], 'An explicit reconsideration condition supports accountable learning.'),
                ('Learning completion is separate from operational authority and certification.', 'What does completing this fictional practicum demonstrate?', 'Assessed learning within the course, subject to separate school gates.', ['Authority to approve government policy.', 'Automatic school certification without its exam.', 'A verified ParaLearn provider integration.'], 'A learning exercise does not confer external powers or satisfy unverified certification requirements.')])])
